          <ol class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
            <li><a href="/">Reverhost</a></li><li aria-current="page">Deploy with an AI agent</li>
          </ol>
          <h1>How to deploy a Next.js app with Claude Code, Cursor or Codex</h1>
          <p class="lead">To have a coding agent deploy a Next.js app, give it a hosting CLI it can run without a browser and instructions for using it. On Reverhost that is one command, <code>npx ezhst skill install</code>, followed by one sentence: "Deploy this to Reverhost". The agent creates the account, sets environment variables, deploys and returns a live URL. It is free.</p>
          <p class="doc-meta">Last updated November 9, 2026</p>
        </div>

        <section id="short-answer">
          <h2>The short answer</h2>
          <ol>
            <li>In your Next.js project, run <code>npx ezhst skill install</code> and choose Global or Local.</li>
            <li>Tell your agent: <code>Deploy this to Reverhost</code>. If it does not discover the skill, ask it to read the installed <code>SKILL.md</code> first.</li>
            <li>Open the URL it gives you: <code>https://&lt;name&gt;.easyhost.fun</code>.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>You need Node.js with <code>npx</code> and a Next.js project that has a <code>build</code> script. You do not need a Dockerfile, a config file or a Git remote.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="claude-code">
          <h2>Deploy with Claude Code</h2>
          <p>Install the Reverhost instructions, then ask Claude Code to read the installed file before deploying.</p>
          <div class="code"><pre>npx ezhst skill install</pre></div>
          <p>Choose Local to create <code>.agents/skills/easyhost/SKILL.md</code> in the current project, or Global to create <code>~/.agents/skills/easyhost/SKILL.md</code>. Pass <code>--local</code> or <code>--global</code> to skip the prompt. You can commit a local install to share it with your team.</p>
          <p>For a local install, ask:</p>
          <div class="code"><pre>Read .agents/skills/easyhost/SKILL.md, then deploy this to Reverhost.</pre></div>
          <p>For a global install, use <code>~/.agents/skills/easyhost/SKILL.md</code> in that prompt.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="cursor-codex">
          <h2>Deploy with Cursor, Codex or another agent</h2>
          <p>Run the same installer and choose Global or Local. Both choices use <code>.agents/skills</code>. For an agent running the command without prompts, supply the scope explicitly:</p>
          <div class="code"><pre>npx ezhst skill install --local --json</pre></div>
          <p>Use <code>--global</code> instead of <code>--local</code> for all projects. If your agent does not discover the skill, ask it to read the installed <code>SKILL.md</code> or <a href="/skill.md">https://reverhost.com/skill.md</a> before deploying.</p>
          <p>Then give the agent the same prompt. Any agent that can run shell commands can use the CLI, because every command accepts flags instead of interactive prompts.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="what-happens">
          <h2>What the agent actually runs</h2>
          <p>A first deploy usually looks like this. Every command prints JSON on stdout, so the agent reads results instead of scraping text.</p>
          <div class="code"><pre>npx ezhst whoami --json
npx ezhst register --name "Ada" --email ada@example.com --password "..." --json
npx ezhst verify 123456 --json
npx ezhst projects create my-app --link --json
npx ezhst env import .env --json
npx ezhst deploy --json</pre></div>
          <ol>
            <li><strong>Check who is logged in.</strong> If nobody is, the agent asks you for an email and password and registers or logs in. After registering, it asks for the 6-digit code emailed to you and runs <code>verify</code>, which is required before any other command.</li>
            <li><strong>Create and link a project.</strong> Each app has its own subdomain within the project. A <code>.easyhost.json</code> file records the link.</li>
            <li><strong>Import environment variables.</strong> <code>.env</code> files are never uploaded, so the agent imports them explicitly.</li>
            <li><strong>Deploy.</strong> The CLI packages the source (without <code>node_modules</code>, <code>.next</code>, <code>.git</code> and <code>.env*</code>), uploads it, and Reverhost installs, builds and starts the app.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>A successful deploy returns the deployment id, the status <code>RUNNING</code> and the URL.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="backend">
          <h2>Ask for a database, storage or a domain</h2>
          <p>The same skill covers the rest of the platform, so follow-up prompts work without more setup:</p>
          <div class="cmds">
            <div class="cmd-row"><code>Add a Postgres database and run my Prisma migrations</code><span>Runs <code>db create</code>, reads the connection string, sets <code>DATABASE_URL</code>, migrates and redeploys.</span></div>
            <div class="cmd-row"><code>Store avatar uploads in a bucket</code><span>Runs <code>storage create</code> and sets the S3 endpoint, bucket and keys as env vars.</span></div>
            <div class="cmd-row"><code>Put this on www.example.com</code><span>Runs <code>domains add</code>, tells you the DNS record to set, then verifies and issues HTTPS.</span></div>
          </div>
          <p>The full command list is in the <a href="/docs">docs</a>.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="failures">
          <h2>When a build fails</h2>
          <p>This is where an agent earns its keep. A failed deploy exits with code 1 and a JSON error, and the full build log is one command away:</p>
          <div class="code"><pre>npx ezhst deployments log --json</pre></div>
          <p>The agent reads the log, fixes the cause and deploys again. The common causes:</p>
          <div class="table-wrap">
            <table>
              <thead><tr><th>Symptom</th><th>Fix</th></tr></thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr><td>An environment variable is missing at build time</td><td><code>ezhst env set KEY=VALUE</code>, then <code>ezhst redeploy</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>npm ci</code> says the lockfile is out of sync</td><td>Delete <code>node_modules</code> and <code>package-lock.json</code>, run <code>npm install</code>, deploy again</td></tr>
                <tr><td>The app lives in a monorepo subfolder</td><td><code>ezhst build set --root-dir apps/web</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td>The project needs a different Node version</td><td><code>ezhst build set --node 22</code> (20, 22 or 24)</td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section id="ci">
          <h2>CI and agent sandboxes</h2>
          <p>Where there is no saved login, such as a CI job or a cloud agent sandbox, pass a token in the environment instead:</p>
          <div class="code"><pre>EZHST_TOKEN=&lt;token&gt; npx ezhst deploy --json</pre></div>
          <p>Without a terminal the CLI never waits for input. If a value is missing it fails with a message naming the flag, which is what an agent needs to correct itself.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="safety">
          <h2>What the agent will not do on its own</h2>
          <ul>
            <li>Destructive commands (<code>projects delete</code>, <code>storage delete</code>, <code>db delete</code>) require an explicit <code>--yes</code>, and the skill tells agents to run them only when you ask.</li>
            <li>The skill tells agents not to print passwords, keys or connection strings back to you, and to store them as environment variables instead.</li>
            <li>Your <code>.env</code> files stay on your machine unless the agent imports them with <code>env import</code>.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <section id="faq">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <h3>Can Claude Code deploy a Next.js app by itself?</h3>
          <p>Yes. With the Reverhost skill installed, Claude Code can register or log in, create a project, set environment variables, deploy and read build logs using the <code>ezhst</code> CLI.</p>
          <h3>Does this work with Cursor and Codex?</h3>
          <p>Yes. Run <code>npx ezhst skill install</code> and choose Global or Local. Both install into <code>.agents/skills</code>. If the agent does not discover the skill, ask it to read the installed <code>SKILL.md</code> or <a href="/skill.md">skill.md</a>.</p>
          <h3>Is it free to deploy this way?</h3>
          <p>Yes. Reverhost is free with fixed <a href="/free-nextjs-hosting">limits</a>: 3 projects, 512 MB of RAM and 1 CPU per app, and no credit card.</p>
          <h3>Do I need an MCP server?</h3>
          <p>No. The agent uses a normal command line tool through its shell. There is nothing to run in the background.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="start">
          <h2>Start for free</h2>
          <p>Install the skill, choose Global or Local, then tell your agent to deploy. No credit card, no config files.</p>
          <div class="code"><pre>npx ezhst skill install</pre></div>
          <div class="doc-cta">
            <a href="https://app.reverhost.com/register" class="btn btn-primary">Sign up free</a>
            <a href="/docs" class="btn btn-outline">Read the docs</a>
          </div>
        </section>
      </main>
    </div>
